REWRITE.update({'The quantities defined in the following sections (Eqs. 1–19)': 'The quantities defined in the following sections (Eqs. 1–19) were computed per fly and light phase (whole record in constant darkness) at two levels. At the observed level, the primary description, the transition matrices are the fitted observed S/Q/W chains (Observed S/Q/W chain), with Q, W and S in the roles of H₁, H₂ and H₃, and the counts are the observed symbol changes. At the hidden level, used as a check, the transition matrices are the fitted phase-specific hidden-Markov-model matrices (K = 3, labelled states), and every count-based quantity is evaluated on each of 200 paths drawn from the posterior distribution of hidden paths and then averaged over paths. Transitions observed in one direction only are handled by the rule-of-three bound (Entropy production rate) rather than by an observability threshold, and fly–phases whose chain carries no net cycle (|J| < 10⁻¹²) are counted and excluded from directional and thermodynamic summaries.', 'The observability gate was replaced by a bound': 'Edges observed in one direction only were handled by a bound. An edge pair traversed in one direction only (count k in one direction, 0 in the other) states that the reverse rate is below the detection limit; at 95% confidence that limit is 3 transitions over the recording (the Poisson zero-count limit, or rule of three). The edge term (k − b) ln(k/b) decreases monotonically in the unobserved count b and vanishes at b = k, so its minimum over the permitted range 0 ≤ b ≤ 3 lies at b* = min(3, k). The term therefore becomes (k − 3) ln(k/3) for k > 3 and 0 otherwise. Substituting b* in both the prefactor and the logarithm makes each such edge term a 95% lower bound given the observed forward count, and the sum non-negative by construction; it coincides with Eq. (3) whenever both directions are observed. The estimator as a whole is not a lower bound on the true rate: with finite counts the Schnakenberg sum is biased upward when the true irreversibility is small (Validation of the inference, item vii). Model-based entropy production per phase was computed from the fitted transition matrix at its stationary distribution, and was reported as infinite when a transition was present in one direction only.', 'and the entropy production, Eq. 3) was evaluated on each sampled path': "At the hidden level, every nonlinear function of the counts (the routing fractions, Eqs. 14–15, and the entropy production, Eq. 3) was evaluated on each sampled path and then averaged over the 200 paths, respecting Jensen's inequality. On a given path a routing fraction required at least 3 routes in its denominator; a fly's value was reported if it was defined on at least half of its paths. Viterbi counts were computed for comparison only. The model-predicted routing fractions (Eq. 16) were computed from the phase matrices P^L and P^D."})
